<?nvs
// The configuration snapshot, as a program: what an operator wrote in
// `nvs.toml` read back through `Core\Config::get`, moved by
// `Core\Config::set` inside this request, and stopped by the ceiling the
// operator kept for themselves.
//
// ADR 0064 § 5 is the API — `get(string): ?string`, `set(string, string):
// bool`, and `restore`/`all` beside them. ADR 0005 is the rule the two
// middle lines exist for: a `Runtime` directive moves freely up to the
// `[limits.hard]` ceiling, and a set above it **returns `false` and leaves
// the value unchanged** rather than throwing. `m6.md`'s *Verify* says the
// same in the other direction, so a version of this program wrapping the
// third call in a `try` has found the rule and not a bug.
//
// The two numbers come from this repository's own `nvs.toml` — `[limits]
// memory = "256M"` is the default and `[limits.hard] memory = "512M"` is
// the ceiling — so `512M` is the last accepted value and `1G` is the first
// refused one, named together the way a bound wants to be.
//
// The four lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

// The operator's default, before this request has touched anything.
echo 'memory=' . (Core\Config::get('memory') ?? 'unset') . "\n";

// Up to the ceiling: accepted, and it takes effect.
if (Core\Config::set('memory', '512M')) {
    echo 'set ok', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'set failed', "\n";
}

// Past it: refused. No throw, no `catch`, just `false`.
if (Core\Config::set('memory', '1G')) {
    echo 'set accepted', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'set refused', "\n";
}

// And the half a bare `false` would not prove: the refusal left the value
// the accepted call put there, not the one it rejected and not the default.
echo 'still=' . (Core\Config::get('memory') ?? 'unset') . "\n";
